---
import { CONTACT } from "../../i18n/locale";
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import { counterpart, pathFor } from "../../i18n/routes";

// Mirrors /confidentialite: same build switch, same facts. The French page is the reference.
const newsletter = Boolean(import.meta.env.PUBLIC_NEWSLETTER);
---

<Page
  title="Privacy"
  description="Your answers stay in your browser: Over There never receives them and only counts answers anonymously, without cookies."
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lead">Last updated: {newsletter ? "18" : "15"} November 2026.</p>

  {newsletter ? (
    <>
      <h2>Without an account, your answers stay with you</h2>
      <p>
        The ranking of the cities is calculated in your browser. As long as you do not save your
        journey or get your results by email, your answers are never sent to us. Your browser keeps
        them (local storage) so that the full questionnaire picks up where you left off; the "Start
        again" button erases them. Only an anonymous counter per answer is sent, as explained below.
      </p>

      <h2>If you save your journey or get your results by email</h2>
      <p>
        Two actions open a space: "Continue later" during the quiz, and "Get it by email" under your
        results. In both cases there is no password: a link sent by email signs you in.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What we keep:</b> your email address, your answers, your journeys and their dates. Your
        origin, your family's country, your ties, your religion and your filters on health and on
        laws against homosexuality, like the places you left out ("Not for me"), are kept only if
        you tick the box that names them, separately, for each journey. Without it, those answers
        stay on your device, even if you give them later.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Why:</b> so that you can find your journey and your full result again, on any device.
        Nothing else.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>On what basis:</b> for "Get it by email", your request: keeping the result is what lets
        us show it to you in full. For "Continue later" and for the sensitive answers, your explicit
        consent, given by ticking the box provided. You can withdraw it at any time by deleting a
        journey or your whole space.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Who can see it:</b> only the Over There team (Big Emotion). Your answers are never sold,
        lent or given to a partner, nor used for advertising.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Where:</b> on our server, hosted in Germany (European Union). Emails are sent from Big
        Emotion's Microsoft 365 mailbox.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>For how long:</b> three years after your last visit, then everything is erased. A journey
        saved but never confirmed through the link you received is erased after 7 days, along with
        the address if its space was never opened. Sign-in links expire: 15 minutes from My space,
        24 hours after "Continue later", 7 days in the results email. The session lasts 30 days.
        Erasure is automatic, every day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Your rights:</b> in <a href={pathFor("space", "en")}>My space</a>, you can see, resume,
        download or delete your journeys, or delete your whole space. For any other request:{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.en}`}>{CONTACT.en}</a>. You can also contact the CNIL, the French
        data protection authority.
      </p>

      <h2>If you react to a city</h2>
      <p>
        <b>What is public:</b> the text of your reaction, the name you choose (otherwise "User" and
        a number), its date and your journey: your top 3, the cities and their percentages, and your
        way of living (a reading inspired by the Big Five; it is not a personality test). Never your
        answers. To keep your journey to yourself, tick "Do not make my top 3 or my personality
        profile public". Your email address is never shown.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What we keep:</b> your address, to check once that it is yours. The link you receive
        publishes the reaction and opens your space; after that, your reactions are published
        straight away. A reaction never confirmed is erased after 7 days.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Your rights:</b> "Delete" under your reaction erases it at once. Your reactions are in
        the file you download from My space, and erased with your space. To report a reaction you
        need to be signed in: a reaction reported by three different people is hidden until we
        review it. We keep who reported what, to count each person only once; it is in your file and
        erased with your space.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Replies and hearts:</b> a reply to a review is public like a reaction, without your
        journey. A heart or another emoji only shows as a number: nobody sees who left it. We keep
        the link between your space and your hearts so that a second tap removes them; they are in
        the file you download from My space and erased with your space.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Translating a review:</b> when a reader asks for a review to be translated, only that
        review's public text is sent to DeepL SE (Germany, European Union), which translates it. It
        is translated once per language and the translation is kept in our database alongside the
        review, and deleted with it. Never the author's name, journey, answers or email address.
        Nothing is sent unless someone presses the button.
      </p>

      <h2>If you write to us with "Your feedback"</h2>
      <p>
        The "Your feedback" tab, at the edge of the screen, is for giving your opinion of the site
        or writing to us. This message is private: unlike the city reviews, it is never published.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>What is sent:</b> your message, its kind (opinion of the site or message), the address of
        the page you write it on (for example "/en/cities/") and your email only if you give it to
        get a reply. Nothing from the quiz: not your answers, not your results, not your journey.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Who reads it:</b> only the Over There team (Big Emotion). It is kept on our server, in
        Germany, and sent to the team's mailbox through Big Emotion's Microsoft 365 mail. If you
        gave your email, we can reply; otherwise nobody can know who wrote it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>How long:</b> 1 year after it is sent, then it is erased automatically. To have it erased
        sooner: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.en}`}>{CONTACT.en}</a>.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <>
      <h2>Your answers stay with you</h2>
      <p>
        The ranking of the cities is calculated in your browser. Your answers are never sent to us
        and are linked to nobody. Your browser keeps them (local storage) so that the full
        questionnaire picks up where you left off; the full questionnaire's "Start again" button
        erases them, as does clearing the site's data in your browser. Only an anonymous counter per
        answer is sent, as explained below.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>Sharing or keeping your results</h2>
  <p>
    The WhatsApp, Facebook and X buttons are plain links: nothing from these networks loads on Over
    There until you click. The click opens their sharing page with your three cities and the link to
    your result, and that network's own rules then apply. Instagram and TikTok go through your
    phone's share menu, with the picture of your result; on a computer, the link is copied. We only
    count which network was chosen, never your result.
  </p>
  {newsletter ? (
    <>
      <p>
        "Share", "Save the picture" and "Print" go through your phone or your printer: Over There
        receives nothing. The shared link holds your way-of-living profile and your cities, placed
        after the "#", a part that browsers do not send to the server.
      </p>
      <p>
        "Get it by email": you give us an address, we send your three cities and their percentages
        to it, and we keep your result in your space, as explained above. The email's button opens
        it, with the full ranking. The email itself only holds your way-of-living profile and your
        cities, never your answers: it goes through Big Emotion's Microsoft 365 mailbox, which sends
        it without keeping a copy in its sent items.
      </p>
      <p>
        The news box is separate: without it, you receive nothing but your results. If you tick it,
        the email's button also confirms your sign-up, which follows the rules in the "News by
        email" section. To prevent abuse, the server counts the emails sent per address over one
        day, without writing them down anywhere.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <p>
      "Share", "Save the picture", "Print" and "Get it by email" go through your phone, your printer
      or your own email: Over There receives nothing. The picture is drawn in your browser. The
      shared message and link hold your way-of-living profile (your five answers about Saturday
      night, discovery, planning, helping out and the unexpected) and your cities with their
      percentages, never your other answers: not your origin, nor your family's country, nor your
      religion, nor your filters. This information is placed after the "#" in the link, a part that
      browsers do not send to the server.
    </p>
  )}

  {newsletter && (
    <>
      <h2>News by email</h2>
      <p>
        If you sign up for news, we keep your email address and nothing else. It is used for one
        thing only: telling you what changes on Over There (new cities, a better questionnaire), one
        email a month at most.
      </p>
      <p>
        The news list is separate from your answers: it only holds addresses. We do not sell it, we
        do not lend it, and we give it to no partner.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your address is kept in a separate list, on our server hosted in Germany (European Union),
        and the emails are sent from Big Emotion's Microsoft 365 mailbox. You confirm your sign-up
        through a link sent by email, valid for 7 days: without that confirmation, your address does
        not join the list and you receive nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every email, starting with the confirmation, holds a link to unsubscribe. It opens a page on
        the site where a button erases your address from the list at once; if you have a space, news
        is switched off there too. Unless you confirm again, your address is erased automatically
        three years after your last confirmation. You can also write to us to see it or have it
        erased: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.en}`}>{CONTACT.en}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>Legal basis: your consent, which you can withdraw at any time.</p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>No trackers, audience measurement without cookies</h2>
  <p>
    {newsletter
      ? 'Over There sets only one cookie, and only if you sign in to My space: it keeps your session for 30 days on this device and does nothing else. "Sign out" erases it. No advertising or social network tools. '
      : "Over There sets no cookies and uses no advertising or social network tools. "}
    The fonts and photos are hosted on the site itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    To find out which questions matter to our readers and where the quiz loses them, we count
    visits, quiz answers and two steps (quiz finished, full questionnaire finished) with Plausible,
    an open-source tool hosted on our own server. Each answer sends an anonymous counter ("this
    question, this answer"), with your project (holidays, settling, investing…) and the question's
    place in your journey, to see where each journey loses its readers. No cookie and no identifier:
    we cannot know who answered what. Your answers about your origin, your family's country, your
    ties, your religion, health and laws against homosexuality are never sent. When you leave a
    place out ("Not for me"), we count only the place on the page and whether it was a city, a
    country or a region; never which one. For reactions, we count in the same way who opens the
    form, who sends it and who goes to read them, with the city and the place on the page; never the
    text, the name or the address. When you react to a city in one tap ("I like it", "It suits me",
    "It doesn't suit me"), we count the city, your reaction and the place on the page; never your
    answers. These reactions are public as counts: our database keeps, for each city, the number of
    each reaction, and nothing about who reacted. Your own reaction stays in your browser (local
    storage), so that you can change it or take it back.
  </p>

  <h2>What the host can see</h2>
  <p>
    As for any website, the server that sends the pages may keep a technical log (IP address, page
    requested, date) for its security and to keep it running. This log never holds your answers.
  </p>

  <h2>Links to our sources</h2>
  <p>
    The scores link to their sources. When you follow a link, you leave Over There: the site you
    visit then applies its own rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    For any question: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.en}`}>{CONTACT.en}</a>. You can also contact the
    CNIL, the French data protection authority (<a href="https://www.cnil.fr">cnil.fr</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    The French version of this page is the reference:{" "}
    <a href={counterpart(Astro.url.pathname)} hreflang="fr" lang="fr">
      Confidentialité
    </a>
    .
  </p>
</Page>
